 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "CONNEMARA. A wild, wet, wonderful corner of Galway: bog, lakes, mountains and sky, in that order of area. About half of it is sky, and the sky has opinions."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "THE TWELVE BENS. Na Beanna Beola, a ring of quartzite peaks, the tallest at 729 metres. In clear weather you can see for forty miles. 'Clear weather' is a rumour."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "KYLEMORE ABBEY. A Victorian castle built in the 1860s by Mitchell Henry for his wife Margaret. Since 1920 it has been home to Benedictine nuns, who keep a very fine walled garden."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "TURF. Cut from the bog and dried in stacks, burnt for heat for centuries. Connemara bogs are protected now. The old sods smell of peat, smoke and a good yarn."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "STONE WALLS. A thousand miles of them across Connemara, all put up by hand with no mortar. The gaps are on purpose. The wind needs to go through or it takes the wall."
  }
 ],
